  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nStat\u00e8 a better approach than the given one: Instead of a heuristic optimization with multiple local searches, use a structured construction inspired by the two-interval solution. This reduces local optima and guarantees mathematical structure. This is better because two-interval solutions have been shown in the continuous case and may generalize well here.\n\nFind the two-interval candidate: [0,a] and [400-200+b,399] with length sum 200, continuously search a and b to minimize max overlap. Why? It leverages known optimality in smaller cases and reduces parameter space considerably. Complexity: O(1) per candidate (n=400 fixed) but parameter space L1= [20,180] (small).\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n- Inputs: None, but global constraints: integral=1, domain [0,2], discrete n=400 for score.\n- Variables: a, b (interval starters) in [20,180] for first interval's length L, and second's length fixed to 200-L.\n- Feasibility: Requires h\u2208[0,1]^400 with integral 1(a).\n- Objective: Minimize. C5 = dx * np.max(np.correlate(h,1-h, mode='full'), but with two-interval structure.\n- Search: Sample a across [20,180] with step 1, build h with [0, L1-1] = 1 and [399-L2+1, 399] = 1.\n- Score: C5(h) using same correlation method as parent code.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\nStep 1: Only n=400: no need to adjust resolution. Start with parent initial if available.\nStep 2: Find best two-interval candidate in [20,180] at sample points.\nStep 3: Use best candidate as initial guess for subsequent optimization.\nStep 4: Run phases 2-8 of parent's code from best candidate.\n- Helper: optimize_lse must use global h_fix to ensure step-function entries and integral constraint. It should use integers in 0 or 1.\n\n4. Search choices:\n- Use only two-interval candidates, no DE/requests for higher resolutions.\n- Sample range for L1: 20 to 180 to avoid empty intervals. This reduces parameter space.\n- Repair not needed as only 0/1 outputs.\n\n5. Computable budget:\n- For two-interval: 200 samples, 400-point correlation each (320,000 f/eval), then allow parent's optimization to use remaining time.\n- Parent optimization's phases: run with budget_s (e.g., 1300 -> approx 1000s left for optimization, as 200 candidates for two-interval takes only ~0.5s).\n\n6. Validation:\n- Check at least 1: [L1,L2] sum to 200.\n- Call compute_c5() finally with candidate's h. Output format: (h_final, c5_bound, n_points) with n_points=400.",
